-- Bold play: reaching a goal with bets that are slightly against you
--
-- You have $50 and need $100 by tonight, and the only way to get it is an even-money bet that you
-- lose a little more often than you win, such as red at roulette. Flat betting puts the same stake
-- on every spin. Bold play bets everything it has, or just enough to reach $100. Dubins and Savage
-- ("How to Gamble If You Must", 1965) showed that when each bet is unfavorable, bold play gives the
-- best chance of reaching the goal. When each bet is favorable, the smallest bets do best instead,
-- and in a fair game every strategy gives the same chance.
--
-- Both systems stop at $0 or $100. `eventually` without a bound covers runs of any length, so the
-- answers are exact. Stakes are whole dollars, spins are independent, and there is no table limit.

-- Flat betting: the same stake on every spin.
system Flat(p : Num, stake : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

-- Bold play: everything you have, or just enough to reach $100.
system Bold(p : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      let stake := min(money, 100 - money)
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

-- American roulette: red wins on 18 of the 38 pockets. From $50 to $100, by stake:
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 5, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 10, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 25, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Bold(18/38, 50) |= eventually money == 100

-- European roulette: red wins on 18 of 37.
#prob Flat(18/37, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Bold(18/37, 50) |= eventually money == 100

-- $1 stakes take about 940 spins on average, so about $940 goes on the table, and the house keeps
-- 2/38 of every dollar bet: an expected loss of about $49.49 of the $50. Bold play bets $50 once.
#expect Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100

-- From $30, bold play bets $30, then goes around the cycle 60, 20, 40, 80 until it wins or loses.
#prob Bold(18/38, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#expect Bold(18/38, 30) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100

-- In a fair game both strategies reach $100 from $30 with probability exactly 3/10.
#prob Bold(1/2, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(1/2, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100

-- A bet whose edge you only roughly know: you win most plausibly 49% of the time, possibly 49.5%
-- or 50%, and just possibly 50.5% if your edge is real. With $1 stakes the chance of doubling
-- depends heavily on which of these is true; with bold play it barely moves.
param win : Num ~= {0.49: 1, 0.495: 0.7, 0.5: 0.4, 0.505: 0.2}

claim flat_doubles : Flat(win, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4
claim bold_doubles : Bold(win, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4
